[38;5;252m[1m## Translation notes[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- The C union type-pun ([38;5;10mfloat[0m → [38;5;10muint32_t[0m) becomes [38;5;10mf32::to_bits[0m, the same bit reinterpretation.[0m[0m
- The addition happens in [38;5;10mu32[0m and is then truncated by [38;5;10mas u16[0m, matching C's promotion-then-cast. I used [38;5;10mwrapping_add[0m so behavior is identical in debug builds too, where a plain [38;5;10m+[0m would panic on overflow instead of wrapping.[0m[0m
- Indexing and shifting are provably safe and need no [38;5;10munsafe[0m: the 9-bit mask bounds [38;5;10mj[0m to [38;5;10m0..=511[0m, and [38;5;10mm__shift[0m holds only [38;5;10m13..=24[0m, always under 32. The body is safe Rust; [38;5;10munsafe[0m appears only on the required [38;5;10m#[unsafe(no_mangle)][0m attribute.[0m[0m
- No bugs were "fixed." Worth flagging that this table-driven float→half conversion truncates the mantissa rather than round-to-nearest-even, and maps overflowing finite values to infinity — that is the C's behavior, reproduced as-is.[0m[0m
